    cards: [
      {
        type: "intro",
        title: "The Fracture-Care Code Space",
        content: `Musculoskeletal codes (**20100-29999**) are the surgery section's largest body-system block. Fracture care is its centerpiece — and the place where new coders most often pick the wrong code.

Three axes define every fracture-treatment CPT code:

1. **Closed vs Open vs Percutaneous fixation** — describes whether the skin/bone was cut.
2. **With vs Without manipulation** — was the bone *reduced* (reset) into alignment?
3. **Type of stabilization** — cast, splint, internal fixation (plates, screws, rods), external fixator.

Combining the first two axes gives **four standard fracture-treatment combinations**. Add stabilization details and you get the precise code. Most fracture-care codes carry a **90-day global period**, meaning all routine follow-up — cast checks, suture removal, X-rays — is included. The **HCPCS Q codes** cover the casting supplies separately.`,
        visual: {
          type: "diagram",
          svg: `<svg viewBox="0 0 1100 1100" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><rect width="1100" height="1100" fill="#1a1a2e"/><text x="550" y="60" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="36" font-weight="bold">Fracture Care: 3 Coding Axes</text><g font-family="sans-serif"><rect x="80" y="130" width="940" height="240" rx="16" fill="#6366f1" opacity="0.85"/><text x="550" y="180" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="28" font-weight="bold">Axis 1 — Approach</text><text x="280" y="240" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="22">CLOSED</text><text x="280" y="280" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="16">no skin cut</text><text x="550" y="240" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="22">PERCUTANEOUS</text><text x="550" y="280" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="16">pins through skin</text><text x="820" y="240" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="22">OPEN</text><text x="820" y="280" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="16">surgical exposure</text><text x="550" y="340" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="16">Each describes how the surgeon reaches the bone</text><rect x="80" y="400" width="940" height="240" rx="16" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.85"/><text x="550" y="450" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="28" font-weight="bold">Axis 2 — Manipulation</text><text x="350" y="520" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="22">WITHOUT</text><text x="350" y="560" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="16">no reduction needed</text><text x="750" y="520" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="22">WITH</text><text x="750" y="560" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="16">bone reset into alignment</text><text x="550" y="615" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="16">"Manipulation" = reduction</text><rect x="80" y="670" width="940" height="240" rx="16" fill="#ec4899" opacity="0.85"/><text x="550" y="720" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="28" font-weight="bold">Axis 3 — Stabilization</text><text x="220" y="790" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20">Cast/</text><text x="220" y="815" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20">Splint</text><text x="430" y="790" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20">Internal</text><text x="430" y="815" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20">fixation</text><text x="640" y="790" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20">External</text><text x="640" y="815" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20">fixator</text><text x="850" y="790" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20">Traction</text><text x="550" y="880" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="16">Determines the specific code within each approach/manipulation combo</text><text x="550" y="960" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">Most fracture codes carry a 90-day global period</text><text x="550" y="1010" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20">All routine follow-up bundled — cast checks, X-rays, suture removal</text></g></svg>`,
          caption: "Three axes — approach, manipulation, stabilization — determine the fracture code."
        }
      },
      {
        type: "concept",
        title: "The Four Treatment Combinations",
        content: `Combine **approach** × **manipulation** and you get the **four standard fracture-treatment combinations** baked into CPT descriptors. Distal radius (Colles') fracture examples:

- **Closed, no manipulation** — bone is already aligned; immobilize only. *e.g., **25600** Closed treatment of distal radial fracture; without manipulation.*
- **Closed, with manipulation** — surgeon reduces fracture through intact skin. *e.g., **25605** Closed treatment of distal radial fracture; with manipulation.*
- **Percutaneous skeletal fixation** — pins/wires placed through skin under image guidance. *e.g., **25606**.*
- **Open, with internal fixation** — surgical exposure, plate/screws. *e.g., **25607-25609** ORIF.*

**Key terminology:** "Open treatment" in CPT means **the fracture site was surgically opened** — NOT the same as an "open fracture" (compound) clinically. A patient with a clinically open (compound) fracture can still receive closed CPT treatment if it's reduced without surgical exposure of the fracture itself.`,
        visual: {
          type: "diagram",
          svg: `<svg viewBox="0 0 1100 1100" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><rect width="1100" height="1100" fill="#1a1a2e"/><text x="550" y="60" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="34" font-weight="bold">The 4 Fracture Treatment Combinations</text><text x="550" y="100" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20" font-style="italic">Example: distal radius (Colles') fracture</text><g font-family="sans-serif"><rect x="60" y="140" width="490" height="380" rx="14" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.85"/><text x="305" y="190" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">CLOSED — no manipulation</text><text x="305" y="240" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20">Bone already aligned</text><text x="305" y="280" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20">Just immobilize</text><rect x="180" y="320" width="250" height="80" rx="10" fill="#1a1a2e" stroke="#ffd700" stroke-width="2"/><text x="305" y="370" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="32" font-weight="bold">25600</text><text x="305" y="450" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="16">cast/splint application included</text><text x="305" y="480" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="16">in initial fracture-care code</text><rect x="560" y="140" width="490" height="380" rx="14" fill="#f59e0b" opacity="0.85"/><text x="805" y="190" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">CLOSED — with manipulation</text><text x="805" y="240" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20">Surgeon reduces fx</text><text x="805" y="280" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20">through intact skin</text><rect x="680" y="320" width="250" height="80" rx="10" fill="#1a1a2e" stroke="#ffd700" stroke-width="2"/><text x="805" y="370" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="32" font-weight="bold">25605</text><text x="805" y="450" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="16">May use conscious sedation</text><text x="805" y="480" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="16">or hematoma block</text><rect x="60" y="550" width="490" height="380" rx="14" fill="#0ea5e9" opacity="0.85"/><text x="305" y="600" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">PERCUTANEOUS fixation</text><text x="305" y="650" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20">Pins/wires through skin</text><text x="305" y="690" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20">under fluoroscopy</text><rect x="180" y="730" width="250" height="80" rx="10" fill="#1a1a2e" stroke="#ffd700" stroke-width="2"/><text x="305" y="780" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="32" font-weight="bold">25606</text><text x="305" y="860" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="16">No surgical opening of</text><text x="305" y="890" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="16">the fracture site itself</text><rect x="560" y="550" width="490" height="380" rx="14" fill="#ef4444" opacity="0.85"/><text x="805" y="600" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">OPEN — ORIF</text><text x="805" y="650" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20">Surgical exposure</text><text x="805" y="690" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20">+ plate / screws</text><rect x="680" y="730" width="250" height="80" rx="10" fill="#1a1a2e" stroke="#ffd700" stroke-width="2"/><text x="805" y="780" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="28" font-weight="bold">25607-25609</text><text x="805" y="860" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="16">Includes hardware placement,</text><text x="805" y="890" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="16">graft if same-site autograft</text><text x="550" y="990" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ec4899" font-size="20" font-style="italic">"Open treatment" = surgical opening of the fx, NOT clinical compound fracture</text><text x="550" y="1040" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="18">A compound fx can still be coded as CLOSED treatment.</text></g></svg>`,
          caption: "Four treatment combos × stabilization choice = the precise fracture code."
        }
      },
      {
        type: "example",
        title: "Casts, Strapping, Global Period & HCPCS Q Codes",
        content: `**Cast and strapping codes (29000-29799)** are NOT a separate billable service when applied as part of initial fracture care — they're **bundled** into the fracture-treatment code (e.g., **25600** includes the first cast).

You DO bill **29000-29799** separately when:
- **Replacing** a cast during the global period (cast removed for skin check, new one applied).
- Applying a cast for a **sprain or other non-fracture condition** (no fracture-care code billed).
- Applying a cast by a **different provider** than the one who did fracture care.

**HCPCS supplies for casting materials** — these are the **Q4001-Q4051** range covering plaster vs fiberglass, body part, and adult vs pediatric. Example: **Q4010** *cast supplies, short arm cast, adult, fiberglass*. Q codes pay for the *material*; CPT pays for the *work*.

**Global period:** Most fracture-care CPT codes are **090** (90-day global). Routine cast checks, X-rays, and suture removal within 90 days are bundled — append **modifier 24** for unrelated E/M, **58** for staged hardware removal.`,
        visual: {
          type: "diagram",
          svg: `<svg viewBox="0 0 1100 1100" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><rect width="1100" height="1100" fill="#1a1a2e"/><text x="550" y="60" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="34" font-weight="bold">Cast Codes, Supplies, Global Period</text><g font-family="sans-serif"><rect x="60" y="120" width="980" height="240" rx="14" fill="#6366f1" opacity="0.85"/><text x="550" y="170" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="26" font-weight="bold">29000-29799 — Cast / Strapping CPT</text><text x="550" y="220" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20">BUNDLED into initial fracture-care code</text><text x="550" y="270" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="22">Bill separately ONLY when:</text><text x="550" y="305" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="18">• Replacement cast in global period</text><text x="550" y="335" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="18">• Sprain / non-fracture care  • Different provider applies</text><rect x="60" y="380" width="980" height="240" rx="14" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.85"/><text x="550" y="430" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="26" font-weight="bold">Q4001-Q4051 — HCPCS Cast Supplies</text><text x="550" y="480" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20">Material billing: plaster vs fiberglass,</text><text x="550" y="510" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20">site (short arm, long leg...), adult vs pediatric</text><text x="550" y="560" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20" font-style="italic">e.g., Q4010 = short arm cast supplies, adult, fiberglass</text><text x="550" y="600" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="18">Q pays for material — CPT pays for work</text><rect x="60" y="640" width="980" height="240" rx="14" fill="#ec4899" opacity="0.85"/><text x="550" y="690" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="26" font-weight="bold">90-Day Global Period</text><text x="550" y="740" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20">Bundled: routine cast checks, X-rays, suture removal</text><text x="550" y="790" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20">Unbundle with modifiers:</text><text x="550" y="825" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="18">24 = unrelated E/M  •  58 = staged hardware removal</text><text x="550" y="860" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="18">78 = unplanned return  •  79 = unrelated procedure</text><rect x="60" y="900" width="980" height="160" rx="14" fill="#1a1a2e" stroke="#ffd700" stroke-width="3"/><text x="550" y="940" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">Sample claim — fresh distal radius fx, manipulated, fiberglass SAC</text><text x="550" y="980" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20">25605  (fracture care, closed, with manipulation)</text><text x="550" y="1015" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20">Q4010  (short arm fiberglass cast supplies, adult)</text><text x="550" y="1050" text-anchor="middle" fill="#10b981" font-size="18">Cast CPT 29075 is NOT billed — bundled into 25605</text></g></svg>`,
          caption: "Cast CPT bundles into fracture care; HCPCS Q codes pay for the supplies."
        }
      },
      {
        type: "quiz",
        title: "Fracture Care Coding Quiz",
        question: "A 28-year-old falls and sustains a closed distal radius fracture. In the ED, an orthopedic surgeon performs a hematoma block, manually reduces the fracture, and applies a short-arm fiberglass cast. The surgeon assumes responsibility for follow-up. What is the most accurate code set?",
        options: [
          { text: "25600 + 29075 + Q4010 (closed treatment without manipulation + cast application + supplies)", correct: false },
          { text: "25605 + Q4010 (closed treatment with manipulation + fiberglass short-arm cast supplies)", correct: true },
          { text: "25607 + Q4010 (open treatment with internal fixation + cast supplies)", correct: false },
          { text: "25605 + 29075 + Q4010 (closed manipulation + cast application + supplies)", correct: false }
        ],
        explanation: "The reduction (manipulation) was performed through intact skin = closed treatment WITH manipulation → 25605. The fiberglass short-arm cast supply is captured by Q4010. CPT 29075 (cast application) is BUNDLED into the fracture-care code 25605 when the same provider delivers initial fracture care, so it is NOT billed separately. 25607 would require surgical exposure of the fracture (open ORIF), which did not occur.",
        visual: {
          type: "diagram",
          svg: `<svg viewBox="0 0 1100 1100" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><rect width="1100" height="1100" fill="#1a1a2e"/><text x="550" y="70" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="34" font-weight="bold">Coding the ED Distal Radius Fx</text><g font-family="sans-serif"><rect x="60" y="120" width="980" height="160" rx="14" fill="#6366f1" opacity="0.3" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="2"/><text x="80" y="160" fill="#ffd700" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">Step 1 — Approach</text><text x="80" y="200" fill="#fff" font-size="20">Hematoma block + manual reduction</text><text x="80" y="240" fill="#10b981" font-size="20">→ CLOSED (no surgical exposure)</text><rect x="60" y="300" width="980" height="160" rx="14" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.3" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="2"/><text x="80" y="340" fill="#ffd700" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">Step 2 — Manipulation</text><text x="80" y="380" fill="#fff" font-size="20">Surgeon reduced the fracture</text><text x="80" y="420" fill="#10b981" font-size="20">→ WITH manipulation</text><rect x="60" y="480" width="980" height="160" rx="14" fill="#f59e0b" opacity="0.3" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="2"/><text x="80" y="520" fill="#ffd700" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">Step 3 — Pick the CPT</text><text x="80" y="565" fill="#fff" font-size="20">Closed + with manipulation, distal radius</text><text x="80" y="605" fill="#10b981" font-size="28" font-weight="bold">→ CPT 25605</text><rect x="60" y="660" width="980" height="160" rx="14" fill="#ec4899" opacity="0.3" stroke="#ec4899" stroke-width="2"/><text x="80" y="700" fill="#ffd700" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">Step 4 — Supplies</text><text x="80" y="740" fill="#fff" font-size="20">Short-arm fiberglass cast, adult</text><text x="80" y="780" fill="#10b981" font-size="24" font-weight="bold">→ HCPCS Q4010</text><rect x="60" y="840" width="980" height="200" rx="14" fill="#1a1a2e" stroke="#ffd700" stroke-width="3"/><text x="550" y="885" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">Final code set</text><text x="550" y="945" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="32" font-weight="bold">25605  +  Q4010</text><text x="550" y="985" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ef4444" font-size="18">Do NOT add 29075 — bundled into 25605</text><text x="550" y="1020" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="18">Global period: 90 days</text></g></svg>`,
          caption: "Closed reduction with manipulation → 25605; Q4010 pays for the cast material."
        }
      },
      {
        type: "application",
        title: "Fracture Care Pitfalls and Pro Moves",
        content: `**Top fracture-care pitfalls and how to avoid them:**

- **Pitfall 1 — Coding "open treatment" because the patient had a compound fracture.** Open in CPT means the *surgeon* opened the fracture site. A compound fx reduced without surgical exposure is **closed treatment**.
- **Pitfall 2 — Billing 29000-29799 with the fracture-care code.** First cast is bundled. Replacement casts within the 90-day global *can* be billed.
- **Pitfall 3 — Forgetting HCPCS Q codes.** Surgeons leave money on the table when supplies aren't claimed.
- **Pitfall 4 — Global-period E/M.** Routine follow-up is bundled. Use **modifier 24** for unrelated visits, **58/78/79** for return procedures.
- **Pro move:** if the **ED physician** does the reduction but **transfers care** to an orthopod, the ED bills E/M only (with **modifier 54** if they applied any fracture care) and the orthopod bills the **definitive fracture-care code with modifier 55** for post-op management. Read the discharge plan carefully.`,
        visual: {
          type: "diagram",
          svg: `<svg viewBox="0 0 1100 1100" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><rect width="1100" height="1100" fill="#1a1a2e"/><text x="550" y="60" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="34" font-weight="bold">Fracture-Care Pitfalls &amp; Pro Moves</text><g font-family="sans-serif"><rect x="60" y="120" width="980" height="170" rx="14" fill="#ef4444" opacity="0.85"/><text x="100" y="170" fill="#ffd700" font-size="30" font-weight="bold">!</text><text x="160" y="170" fill="#fff" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">Pitfall 1 — Coding "open" because clinically compound</text><text x="160" y="210" fill="#fff" font-size="18">CPT "open treatment" = surgeon opens the fx site</text><text x="160" y="245" fill="#fff" font-size="18">Compound fx reduced without exposure = CLOSED treatment</text><rect x="60" y="310" width="980" height="170" rx="14" fill="#f59e0b" opacity="0.85"/><text x="100" y="360" fill="#ffd700" font-size="30" font-weight="bold">!</text><text x="160" y="360" fill="#fff" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">Pitfall 2 — Billing 29000-29799 with fracture care</text><text x="160" y="400" fill="#fff" font-size="18">First cast bundled. Replacements during global → billable</text><text x="160" y="435" fill="#fff" font-size="18">Different provider → different rules</text><rect x="60" y="500" width="980" height="170" rx="14" fill="#8b5cf6" opacity="0.85"/><text x="100" y="550" fill="#ffd700" font-size="30" font-weight="bold">!</text><text x="160" y="550" fill="#fff" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">Pitfall 3 — Missing HCPCS Q codes</text><text x="160" y="590" fill="#fff" font-size="18">Q4001-Q4051 cover cast materials</text><text x="160" y="625" fill="#fff" font-size="18">Always pair with the cast/fracture work CPT</text><rect x="60" y="690" width="980" height="170" rx="14" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.85"/><text x="100" y="740" fill="#ffd700" font-size="30" font-weight="bold">★</text><text x="160" y="740" fill="#fff" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">Pro Move — Split-care modifiers 54 / 55</text><text x="160" y="780" fill="#fff" font-size="18">ED reduces, transfers to ortho → ED uses 54 (intra-op only)</text><text x="160" y="815" fill="#fff" font-size="18">Ortho bills the same fracture-care CPT with 55 (post-op mgmt)</text><rect x="60" y="880" width="980" height="170" rx="14" fill="#0ea5e9" opacity="0.85"/><text x="100" y="930" fill="#ffd700" font-size="30" font-weight="bold">★</text><text x="160" y="930" fill="#fff" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">Pro Move — Global-period modifiers</text><text x="160" y="970" fill="#fff" font-size="18">24 = unrelated E/M  •  58 = staged related</text><text x="160" y="1005" fill="#fff" font-size="18">78 = unplanned return to OR  •  79 = unrelated procedure</text></g></svg>`,
          caption: "Avoid the four classic pitfalls; deploy 54/55 split-care modifiers when transferring management."
        }
      }
    ]
